# The cases named in the issue's own table. Every one is somewhere a parser or
# a writer can be quietly wrong, and quietly is the operative word: each of
# these round-trips to *something*, just not always the same molecule.

# Sixty aromatic carbons, twelve five-membered rings, thirty-two ring closures
# live at once. Breaks ring-closure bookkeeping that assumes a small digit pool.
c12c3c4c5c1c6c7c8c2c9c1c3c2c4c3c5c4c6c5c7c6c7c8c9c8c1c2c3c1c4c5c2c6c7c8c12 buckyball
# Tetrahedral stereo. Dropping @@ yields a valid molecule that is the wrong one.
N[C@@H](C)C(=O)O alanine
# Double-bond geometry, and the pair that must not collapse into each other.
F/C=C/F trans-difluoroethene
F/C=C\F cis-difluoroethene
# Two fragments, one record. A parser that returns the first component is wrong
# without ever failing — and a *writer* that returns the first component is
# worse, which is why #191 fixed both ends at once.
[Na+].[Cl-] sodium-chloride
# Aromatic nitrogen carrying the ring's hydrogen. The [nH] is load-bearing:
# without it the ring has no valid Kekule form.
c1cc[nH]c1 pyrrole
# Isotope. Same connectivity, same charge, different molecule.
[13CH4] carbon-13-methane
